WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.980
Vietnam honored 731 war dead today, as a national search race is aging relatives to name them. This is Omni View Asia Pacific, Episode 22. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. Ho Chi Minh City held the rights on October 14th for people still missing from the wars with France and the United States. The Straits Times reports that tens of thousands stood along the streets, some with white flowers, some praying, while buses carried flag-covered caskets out to a cemetery for fallen soldiers.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.540
The military says the graves, individual and mass, were in Le Thierryng Park, and the

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:38.940
fighters died when U.S.-backed forces crushed a communist uprising in 1968.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.360
Deputy Mayor Nguyen Mon Quang said the city will not overlook any opportunity or method

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.020
to keep searching for fallen comrades.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:51.060
He also said the war's ferocity, and the years since, mean most of those recovered in

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.000
this phase still have no name and no hometown on record.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.900
State media named one exception, squad leader Huynh Van Quen, who fought in 1968,

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.460
matched in July by a scrap of paper with his name and by DNA from his relatives.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.120
Deputy Prime Minister Pham T. Thanh Trah called the goal of recovering 7,000 sets of remains

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:15.420
by July 2027, a political task of special importance and a command from the heart.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:21.640
Up to 1.2 million Vietnamese soldiers are estimated killed between 1945 and 1975.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.980
230,000 graves nationwide hold unidentified soldiers.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:31.260
More than 1,500 people are searching old battlefields in Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.460
While other teams sample those graves and run DNA tests on 18,000 samples of remains,

00:01:37.681 --> 00:01:42.081
a sole court has sent an independent lawmaker to prison over cash for a party nomination.

00:01:42.761 --> 00:01:47.041
Jan Ha purports the Seoul Central District Court gave Kong Sun Wu two and a half years

00:01:47.041 --> 00:01:50.381
on Wednesday and ordered her to forfeit 100 million won.

00:01:50.381 --> 00:01:52.801
on, about $75,000 US dollars.

00:01:53.041 --> 00:01:58.201
She was charged with taking that sum in January 2022 from former Seoul City Councilor Kim

00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:02.661
Kyung, in exchange for a democratic party nomination before that year's local elections.

00:02:03.161 --> 00:02:06.681
She sat then on the party's nomination committee for Seoul City Councilors.

00:02:07.161 --> 00:02:08.981
Kim was selected and Kim was elected.

00:02:09.301 --> 00:02:14.081
The court gave Kim one year and gave a former aide to Kong eight months, suspended for

00:02:14.081 --> 00:02:15.661
two years for setting up the meeting.

00:02:15.661 --> 00:02:20.541
Judges said the defendant's traded illegal political funds, hurt the fairness of the election,

00:02:20.961 --> 00:02:25.781
and severely damaged public trust. Kong had been in this ruling Democratic Party and left at

00:02:25.781 --> 00:02:29.741
the start of this year, after a recording emerged of her discussing the money with former

00:02:29.741 --> 00:02:35.423
floor leader Kim Byung-ki. Sopong has a dollar figure on a grand prix that was never meant to be

